KNN Model (K-Nearest Neighbors)
🔹 Definition
K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) is a supervised machine learning algorithm used for classification and regression.
It predicts the output based on the K closest data points (neighbors) in the dataset.
🔹 How KNN Works (Step-by-Step)
Choose a value of K (number of neighbors).
Calculate the distance between the new data point and all existing data points.
Common distance: Euclidean Distance
Select the K nearest points.
For:
Classification → Take the majority class.
Regression → Take the average value.
🔹 Example (Simple)
Suppose you want to classify whether a fruit is Apple 🍎 or Orange 🍊 based on:
Weight
Color
If K = 3:
Find 3 nearest fruits
If 2 are Apples and 1 is Orange → New fruit = Apple
🔹 Formula (Euclidean Distance)
𝑑=(𝑥2−𝑥1)2+(𝑦2−𝑦1)2
d=(x2−x1)2+(y2−y1)2
	​This calculates distance between two points.
🔹 Advantages
✅ Simple and easy to understand
✅ No training phase (lazy learner)
✅ Works well for small datasets
🔹 Disadvantages
❌ Slow for large datasets
❌ Sensitive to irrelevant features
❌ Choosing wrong K affects accuracy
🔹 Applications
Medical diagnosis
Recommendation systems
Pattern recognition
Image classification
🔹 Conclusion (Short Note)
KNN is a simple distance-based algorithm that classifies or predicts data by looking at the nearest neighbors. It is easy to implement but may become slow with large datasets.

